european union agriculture ministers are  holding extraordinary talks in brussels  to discuss ways to help farmers hit by  russia's ban on imports of certain fresh  produce from the EU in august russia  announced a 12 month ban on mostly  perishable fruit and vegetables meat  fish and dairy products after the EU  imposed sanctions on Moscow over the  conflict in eastern Ukraine Russia's  band could hit EU exports worth more  than 5 billion euros several EU  technical meetings have been held since  August sixth when the band was announced  to define emergency support measures for  affected fruit and vegetable producers  in an immediate response to Russia's  move the EU set up an interim budget of  125 million euros and support and  earmarked more than 30 million euros in  emergency aid for peaches and nectarines  The ministers want to work out exactly  what consequences the ban will have on  European agricultural products what  produce should be eligible for EU  support and what kind of budget will be  needed to help struggling farmers to  protect farmers livelihoods countries  outside the EU have been encouraged not  to offer Russia cheap produce to replace  European products while the ban is in  place  Oh
 

